From 498aaee254c082179eac5d154fa0f7df39d719a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:34:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/17] feat(gateway): expose Prometheus metrics at /metrics

The gateway is the single entry point for the resource API and for
streaming inference, but it exported nothing: request rates, latency,
time to first token, upstream failures and resolver behaviour were
only visible by grepping logs.

Add a small standard-library registry for the Prometheus text format
(internal/metrics, with a strict parser for tests) and instrument the
gateway with it: request counts and latency by route class and status,
time to first byte of successful streams, upstream errors by upstream
(control plane, instance) and kind, rate-limit rejections, resolver
lookups (hit, miss, coalesced) and the health state of the instances
in the resolver cache. Label values are route classes, status codes
and fixed enums only; route classes come from the route table via
ServeMux.Handler, never from the path. Each family is capped at 200
series with an "overflow" series behind it as a backstop.

/metrics is registered next to the probes, outside the API chain: it
needs no credentials and is not rate limited, so it only carries
aggregate data with no tenants, services, paths or instance addresses.
Like the probes it should not be routed through a public ingress.
Observe wraps Recovery so that recovered panics count as 500s, and
requests cancelled by the client are not counted as upstream errors.
